Mismatches between the impedances of the antenna and receiver result in partial reflections of both the power incoming from the sky and the noise power generated by the amplifier, shown in Fig. 1. These reflections can lead to standing waves and other spectral distortions in the measured output power that complicate the interpretation of the signal. These systematic effects (specifically, impedance mismatch-induced standing waves and spectral distortions) primarily arise from impedance mismatches between antenna and receiver components, causing standing waves and frequency-dependent distortions in the measured signal.Fig. 1
A schematic overview explaining the core challenge in radiometer calibration. An impedance mismatch between the receiver and the source (usually a radio antenna) causes standing waves between the receiver and source. This adds power to the output signal and must be corrected for via calibration. The reference plane for all measurements and equations is at the LNA input. Internal calibration loads are switched in place of the antenna. Traditional Dicke switching assumes the receiver response is identical for both antenna and reference load, which breaks down when impedance mismatches differ between sources. \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
